css='body{margin:0;background:#f5f0e8;color:#28232c;font:18px/1.65 Georgia,serif}main{max-width:1180px;margin:auto;padding:38px 28px}h1{font-size:48px;line-height:1.1}p{max-width:80ch}a{color:#705079}nav{display:flex;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap}img{width:100%;height:auto;display:block}figure{margin:28px 0}figcaption,small{font-size:15px}figcaption{padding:10px 0}.pair{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:18px}.pair figure{margin:0}details{border:1px solid #cdc5bd;padding:16px;margin:18px 0}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:bold}.diffwrap{overflow:auto}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse}td,th{padding:10px;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid #ccc}.diff{font:12px/1.45 monospace}.diff td{padding:4px}.diff_header{background:#e3ddd4}.diff_add{background:#d3e8d5}.diff_sub{background:#f1d3d2}.diff_chg{background:#f3e6ae}@media(max-width:700px){h1{font-size:34px}.pair{grid-template-columns:1fr}}'
page='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Change — what a reading keeps</title><style>'''+css+'''</style><main><p>Ada Research · 25 September 2026</p><h1>What a reading keeps</h1><p>A line reports a fall while the curve at its beginning rises. A total returns to zero after a climb. A trail draws a crossing that its particle never made. Change already has a strong thread; this pass gives its comparisons clearer locations and brings the other works within reach of the reading.</p><nav><a href="#reading">Exact text differences</a><a href="/compose/sources?map=Change_Intro&amp;doc=final">Read the current chapter</a><a href="/book-tasks/review?seq=change">Review comments</a><a href="DECISIONS.md">Decisions and evidence</a></nav><figure><img src="intro/change-hall.png" alt="The arch and its signed strips inside the museum, with neighboring workbenches"><figcaption>The bridge returns to its starting height. The climb and descent remain there to follow.</figcaption></figure><h2>Two distances, two readings</h2><div class="pair"><figure><img src="intro/two-readings-wide.png" alt="The first museum workbench with a green line sloping down across a crest"><figcaption>The broad interval crosses the crest.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="intro/two-readings-near.png" alt="The same museum camera with a shorter interval and a second slope plot above the curve"><figcaption>Two STEP presses bring the green sample closer. DERIVATIVE reveals the gold tangent and the slope plot above.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The prose now places that second plot above the curve. Across the hall, the moving puck offers a way to bring time into the comparison. Its arrow turns with the path; the suggested claim that its length is the puck’s speed does not match the implementation, so it has not entered the chapter.</p><h2>An earlier answer can stay</h2><figure><img src="intro/four-held-eight-live.png" alt="Eight live blue rectangles beside four held rectangles inside the museum"><figcaption>Eight live intervals on the left; four held on the right. The console and neighbouring works remain in the view.</figcaption></figure><p>The text names the full cycle back to MID. It also points to the pump, staircase, gutter boats and swaying bowl as comparisons to return to. Those supporting encounters are already in the hall.</p><h2>The drawn route and the travelled route</h2><figure><img src="flow/flow-field-near.png" alt="The Flow Field canvas seen from beside its table, with arrows, coloured trails and FIELD HELD label"><figcaption>The field holds while the particles move. Long strokes connect positions on opposite sides of the frame.</figcaption></figure><p>The opening now locates FIELD HELD above the far edge. The next calculation takes the address produced by the last movement. At the boundary, recording those addresses can draw a journey through the middle that never happened. The photograph makes that seam visible.</p><details><summary>Other encounters, and the remaining headset check</summary><figure><img src="flow/grand-zoetrope.png" alt="The Grand Zoetrope inside the Flow Field hall"><figcaption>Twelve poses around a turning drum. The text invites a comparison with the particle’s return.</figcaption></figure><p>The two bench readouts still need to be read in a headset without leaning. These standing console views preserve their existing type and controls; they are not proof of headset legibility. Review item 002 remains open.</p><div class="pair"><figure><img src="intro/rate-console.png" alt="Closer standing view of the rate console"><figcaption>The rate console.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="intro/accumulation-console.png" alt="Closer standing view of the accumulation console"><figcaption>The accumulation console.</figcaption></figure></div></details><p>Eleven review items resolved; one retained for a headset walk. Both final.md chapters and the current Change capture are updated. Three photographs enter the book. The maps, artifact roles and companion manuscripts are retained.</p><details><summary>Checks and image provenance</summary><p><a href="intro/report.json">15 Change_Intro checks</a> · <a href="flow/report.json">12 Flow_Field checks</a>. Native museum loads and supplied control events pass, with normal process exits and no GDScript errors. Existing engine warnings remain in the logs. These are desktop GL compatibility captures, not tracked-headset tests. The first field overview is retained in <a href="flow-first-view/flow-field-held.png">its original view</a>.</p><p><a href="verification.json">Publication checks</a> · <a href="figure-provenance.json">Unmodified figure provenance</a> · <a href="queue-verification.json">Review queue preservation</a>.</p></details><h2 id="reading">Exact changes in final.md</h2>'''+''.join(parts)+'''<h2>Comment by comment</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Review item</th><th>Status</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''+''.join(rows)+'''</tbody></table></main></html>'''
(o/'index.html').write_text(page,encoding='utf-8');print('Published:',len(checks),'scoped checks passed; 11 decisions resolved; 1 headset check open; 3 native book figures.')
